Java Inheritance — Complete Notes
1. Introduction to Inheritance

Inheritance is one of the most important concepts in Object-Oriented Programming (OOP). Java uses inheritance to allow one class to acquire the properties and behaviors of another class.

In simple words, inheritance means creating a new class from an existing class.

The existing class is called the parent class, superclass, or base class.

The new class is called the child class, subclass, or derived class.

For example, suppose we have a class called Animal.

class Animal {
    String name;

    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Animal is eating");
    }
}

Now suppose we create a class called Dog.

class Dog extends Animal {
    void bark() {
        System.out.println("Dog is barking");
    }
}

Here, Dog inherits the name variable and eat() method from Animal.

The extends keyword is used to create inheritance between classes.

class Dog extends Animal

The relationship can be represented as:

Animal
   |
   ↓
  Dog

A dog is an animal, so inheritance represents an IS-A relationship.

Dog IS-A Animal
Cat IS-A Animal
Car IS-A Vehicle
Student IS-A Person

Inheritance helps programmers avoid writing the same code repeatedly.

2. Why Do We Need Inheritance?

Consider the following classes:

class Dog {
    String name;
    int age;

    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Eating");
    }

    void sleep() {
        System.out.println("Sleeping");
    }

    void bark() {
        System.out.println("Barking");
    }
}

Now suppose we create a Cat.

class Cat {
    String name;
    int age;

    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Eating");
    }

    void sleep() {
        System.out.println("Sleeping");
    }

    void meow() {
        System.out.println("Meowing");
    }
}

There is duplicate code.

Both Dog and Cat have:

String name;
int age;

void eat()
void sleep()

Instead, we can create a common parent class.

class Animal {
    String name;
    int age;

    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Eating");
    }

    void sleep() {
        System.out.println("Sleeping");
    }
}

Then:

class Dog extends Animal {
    void bark() {
        System.out.println("Barking");
    }
}

And:

class Cat extends Animal {
    void meow() {
        System.out.println("Meowing");
    }
}

Now common properties and methods are written only once.

This provides:

Code reusability
Less duplicate code
Easier maintenance
Better organization
Support for method overriding
Support for runtime polymorphism
Better representation of real-world relationships
3. Basic Syntax of Inheritance

The general syntax is:

class Child extends Parent {
    // child class members
}

Example:

class Vehicle {
    void start() {
        System.out.println("Vehicle starts");
    }
}

class Car extends Vehicle {
    void drive() {
        System.out.println("Car is driving");
    }
}

Main method:

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Car c = new Car();

        c.start();
        c.drive();
    }
}

Output:

Vehicle starts
Car is driving

The Car object can access the inherited start() method and its own drive() method.

4. Important Terminology

There are several important terms associated with inheritance.

Parent Class

The class whose properties are inherited.

Example:

class Animal {
}

Animal is the parent class.

Child Class

The class that inherits from another class.

class Dog extends Animal {
}

Dog is the child class.

Superclass

Another name for the parent class.

Subclass

Another name for the child class.

Base Class

Another name for the parent class.

Derived Class

Another name for the child class.

5. Types of Inheritance in Java

There are mainly five commonly discussed inheritance structures:

Single inheritance
Multilevel inheritance
Hierarchical inheritance
Multiple inheritance
Hybrid inheritance

However, Java does not support multiple inheritance using classes.

Java supports:

Single inheritance
Multilevel inheritance
Hierarchical inheritance

Multiple and hybrid inheritance can be achieved using interfaces.

6. Single Inheritance

Single inheritance means one child class inherits from one parent class.

Structure:

Parent
   |
   ↓
Child

Example:

class Animal {

    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Eating");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {

    void bark() {
        System.out.println("Barking");
    }
}

Main:

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Dog d = new Dog();

        d.eat();
        d.bark();
    }
}

Output:

Eating
Barking

Here:

Animal → Dog

This is single inheritance.

7. Multilevel Inheritance

Multilevel inheritance occurs when a class inherits from another child class.

Example:

Animal
   |
   ↓
Dog
   |
   ↓
Puppy

Code:

class Animal {

    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Eating");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {

    void bark() {
        System.out.println("Barking");
    }
}

class Puppy extends Dog {

    void play() {
        System.out.println("Playing");
    }
}

Main:

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Puppy p = new Puppy();

        p.eat();
        p.bark();
        p.play();
    }
}

Output:

Eating
Barking
Playing

The Puppy class indirectly inherits from Animal.

Therefore:

Puppy → Dog → Animal

This is multilevel inheritance.

8. Hierarchical Inheritance

Hierarchical inheritance occurs when multiple child classes inherit from the same parent class.

Structure:

          Animal
         /      \
        ↓        ↓
      Dog       Cat

Example:

class Animal {

    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Eating");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {

    void bark() {
        System.out.println("Barking");
    }
}

class Cat extends Animal {

    void meow() {
        System.out.println("Meowing");
    }
}

Main:

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Dog d = new Dog();
        d.eat();
        d.bark();

        Cat c = new Cat();
        c.eat();
        c.meow();
    }
}

Both Dog and Cat inherit from Animal.

9. Multiple Inheritance

Multiple inheritance means a child class inherits from more than one parent class.

For example:

Parent1       Parent2
   \           /
    \         /
      Child

Java does not allow this using classes.

The following code is invalid:

class A {
    void show() {
        System.out.println("A");
    }
}

class B {
    void display() {
        System.out.println("B");
    }
}

class C extends A, B {
}

This produces a compilation error.

Java avoids multiple inheritance with classes mainly because of ambiguity.

10. Diamond Problem

Suppose Java allowed:

       A
      / \
     B   C
      \ /
       D

Suppose both B and C have a method called show().

class B extends A {
    void show() {
        System.out.println("B");
    }
}

class C extends A {
    void show() {
        System.out.println("C");
    }
}

If D inherits from both B and C, then:

D d = new D();
d.show();

Which method should execute?

B.show()

or

C.show()

This creates ambiguity.

This is commonly called the Diamond Problem.

Java avoids this problem by not allowing multiple inheritance through classes.

11. Multiple Inheritance Using Interfaces

Java can achieve multiple inheritance-like behavior using interfaces.

Example:

interface A {

    void show();
}

interface B {

    void display();
}

class C implements A, B {

    public void show() {
        System.out.println("A method");
    }

    public void display() {
        System.out.println("B method");
    }
}

Main:

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        C obj = new C();

        obj.show();
        obj.display();
    }
}

Output:

A method
B method

A class can implement multiple interfaces:

class C implements A, B, D {
}

Therefore Java supports multiple inheritance through interfaces.

12. Hybrid Inheritance

Hybrid inheritance is a combination of two or more types of inheritance.

For example:

       A
      / \
     B   C
      \ /
       D

Java does not support hybrid inheritance using classes because it can create ambiguity.

However, combinations of classes and interfaces can be used to model similar structures.

13. extends Keyword

The extends keyword is used when one class inherits another class.

Example:

class Animal {
    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Eating");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
}

Here:

extends Animal

means that Dog inherits from Animal.

A class can extend only one class directly.

Valid:

class B extends A {
}

Invalid:

class C extends A, B {
}
14. What Is Inherited?

A child class can access inherited members according to their access modifiers.

For example:

class Parent {

    int x = 10;

    void show() {
        System.out.println("Parent");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {

    void display() {
        System.out.println(x);
        show();
    }
}

The child can access x and show().

However, inheritance does not mean that every parent member is directly accessible.

15. Private Members and Inheritance

Private members belong only to the class where they are declared.

Example:

class Parent {

    private int x = 10;
}

class Child extends Parent {

    void show() {
        // System.out.println(x);
    }
}

The child cannot directly access x.

However, the parent can provide public/protected methods to access it.

Example:

class Parent {

    private int x = 10;

    public int getX() {
        return x;
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {

    void show() {
        System.out.println(getX());
    }
}

This is commonly used with encapsulation.

16. Protected Members

The protected access modifier is particularly useful with inheritance.

Example:

class Parent {

    protected int x = 100;
}

class Child extends Parent {

    void show() {
        System.out.println(x);
    }
}

Output:

100

A child class can access protected members inherited from its parent.

17. Constructors and Inheritance

Constructors are not inherited by child classes.

However, when a child object is created, the parent constructor is executed first.

Example:

class Parent {

    Parent() {
        System.out.println("Parent constructor");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {

    Child() {
        System.out.println("Child constructor");
    }
}

Main:

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Child c = new Child();
    }
}

Output:

Parent constructor
Child constructor

The parent constructor runs before the child constructor.

18. Constructor Execution Order

For multilevel inheritance:

GrandParent
     |
     ↓
Parent
     |
     ↓
Child

When a child object is created:

Child c = new Child();

constructors execute in this order:

GrandParent constructor
Parent constructor
Child constructor

This happens because the object must initialize the parent portion before initializing the child portion.

19. super Keyword

The super keyword refers to the immediate parent class.

It can be used to:

Access parent variables
Call parent methods
Call parent constructors
20. super for Accessing Parent Variables

Example:

class Parent {

    int x = 10;
}

class Child extends Parent {

    int x = 20;

    void show() {

        System.out.println(x);
        System.out.println(super.x);
    }
}

Main:

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Child c = new Child();

        c.show();
    }
}

Output:

20
10

x refers to the child variable.

super.x

refers to the parent variable.

21. super for Calling Parent Method

Suppose a child overrides a parent method.

class Parent {

    void show() {
        System.out.println("Parent");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {

    void show() {
        System.out.println("Child");

        super.show();
    }
}

Main:

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Child c = new Child();

        c.show();
    }
}

Output:

Child
Parent
